course: religion-ethics
lesson: 81
title: Why honesty matters: trust holds everything together
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Trust is believing that someone's words are true and that they will do what they say; honesty is what makes trust possible.
explain: Think of Ollie, who tells the whole class, "My dad plays for Arsenal, swear down." By Friday everyone knows it isn't true, and now nobody believes him even about his new puppy.
explain: Trust is built from many true words and can be lost with one big lie; after that even the true words sound suspect, like the boy who cried wolf.
explain: Christians believe God is truthful and cannot lie (Titus 1:2), and the commandment "You shall not bear false witness against your neighbour" (Exodus 20:16) guards the trust between neighbours.
explain: People of other faiths and of none agree: friends, families, shops and teams only work when words can be relied on, even when nobody is checking.
fact: Trust is built from many true words and can be lost with one big lie; afterwards even true words sound suspect.
fact: The commandment "You shall not bear false witness against your neighbour" (Exodus 20:16) guards the trust between neighbours.
fact: Paul wrote, "Put off falsehood and speak truthfully to your neighbour, for we are all members of one body" (Ephesians 4:25).
fact: A mistake told is a problem shared; a mistake hidden becomes a lie on top of a mistake (Proverbs 12:22: the Lord "delights in people who are trustworthy").
fact: Honesty that only works when someone is watching is not honesty yet; Jesus said, "Whoever can be trusted with very little can also be trusted with much" (Luke 16:10).
fact: Plato told of the ring of Gyges, which made its wearer invisible, and asked whether you would still be honest if nobody could ever see you.
fact: Jesus taught, "Let your 'Yes' be 'Yes', and your 'No', 'No'" (Matthew 5:37): an honest person's plain word is enough, without "I swear" or "no cap" ("no cap" is slang for "no lie", and "cap" means a lie).
fact: Scientists find most children start trying lies around age 3 or 4, and learn honesty as patient grown-ups help them; Adam blaming Eve and Eve blaming the serpent (Genesis 3:12-13) is the Bible's first hide-and-blame story.
fact: In Aesop's fable the shepherd boy cries "Wolf!" for fun, and when the wolf really comes nobody believes him; a fable is a made-up story with a lesson.
fact: Proverbs 11:13 says, "A gossip betrays a confidence, but a trustworthy person keeps a secret", and Christians see faithfulness as a fruit of the Holy Spirit (Galatians 5:22).
fact: Honest shops last because buyers come back; Proverbs 11:1 says, "The Lord detests dishonest scales, but accurate weights find favour with him."
fact: Christians believe God cannot lie (Titus 1:2) and Jesus called himself "the way and the truth and the life" (John 14:6).
fact: Hindus say "Satyameva jayate", "Truth alone triumphs", now India's national motto, and one of the five Buddhist precepts is to avoid false speech.
fact: Grandad's old saying «a man's word is his bond» means a promise could be relied on like a signed agreement; because people were known to keep their word, a deal could be sealed with just a handshake.
fact: A lie can hurt more than the thing it hid: a cup can be replaced, but broken trust must be rebuilt, and that takes time.
exercise: Why does one big lie make people doubt someone's true words too? | Because trust is lost, so everything they say now sounds suspect | Because people forget which things were true and which were not, so they give up believing anything | Because big lies are illegal and everyone must report them | Because the true words were probably also lies all along
exercise: Ollie said his dad plays for Arsenal, and it wasn't true. What happened when he told the truth about his puppy? | Nobody believed him, because his big lie had spent their trust | Everybody believed him, because puppies are easy to check | The teacher gave him a prize for telling the truth at last | The class forgot all about the Arsenal lie by the next day
exercise: Which commandment guards the trust between neighbours? | You shall not bear false witness against your neighbour | You shall not make a carved image for yourself | Remember the Sabbath day to keep it holy | You shall not steal your neighbour's ox, his donkey or anything that is his
exercise: Why do many parents care more about the truth than about the mistake? | A mistake told can be fixed together; a hidden one becomes a lie too | Parents secretly enjoy hearing about all the silly mistakes that children make every day | Mistakes do not matter at all as long as nothing got broken | Parents are only angry if a neighbour or teacher finds out
exercise: Jamal fills in his reading log for pages he never read, because "Miss never checks". What is wrong with that? | Honesty that only works when someone is watching is not honesty yet | Nothing, a reading log is only a small piece of paper | It only matters if Miss actually checks it one day and finds out what he did | The only problem is that his handwriting might look rushed
exercise: What did Plato's story of the ring of Gyges ask? | Would you still be honest if nobody could ever see you? | Would you still be brave if you had a magic sword? | Would you share a treasure if you found it alone? | Would you keep a ring that someone had lost?
exercise: Why could Grandad once seal a deal with just a handshake? | Because people were known to keep their word, so no contract was needed | Because paper had not been invented in Grandad's day | Because the law then said that a handshake was stronger than any written contract | Because nobody bought or sold anything important back then
exercise: What did Jesus teach about "I swear" and "no cap" in Matthew 5:37? | Let your 'Yes' be 'Yes', and your 'No', 'No' | Always swear on something when you want people to believe you | Swear only on Sundays and on holy days, never on school days | Say 'no cap' at the end to show everyone that you really mean it
exercise: Alfie, 4, says the dog ate the biscuit, with chocolate round his mouth. What do scientists say about small children's lies? | Many start trying lies at 3 or 4, and learn honesty with patient grown-ups | Children who lie at 4 will always grow up to be liars, however patiently grown-ups teach them | Small children never lie, they only imagine things that happened | Children only learn to lie from watching cartoons on television
exercise: Leo's cousin yells "Shark!" three times as a joke. What will happen if a shark really comes? | People may not believe him, like the boy who cried wolf | The lifeguard will believe him more because he shouted loudest | Everyone will be ready, because he gave them good practice | Nothing, because jokes never change what people believe
exercise: Priya is always at the gate at 8 when she says she will. What does that make her? | A trustworthy friend whose words you can rely on | A boring friend who never does anything fun or surprising | A lucky friend who just happens to live right next to the gate | A strict friend who will tell on you for being late
exercise: Why do honest shops do better in the long run? | Cheated customers never come back, and honest ones tell their friends | Honest shops always charge the most, so they make more money | The law gives honest shops free bikes to sell every year | Customers cannot tell honest shops from dishonest ones anyway
exercise: Why do Christians see truthfulness as part of faith, not just manners? | They believe God is truth and cannot lie, so honesty is becoming like God | They believe God only listens to people who never make mistakes | They believe lying is only wrong inside a church building, but fine outside it in ordinary life | They believe manners are more important than faith anyway
exercise: Sam's mum says, "I'm not angry about the cup. I'm sad that I can't believe you now." What does she mean? | The lie broke her trust, which is harder to mend than a cup | She liked the cup much more than she likes Sam | She wants Sam to go and buy her a more expensive cup with his own pocket money | She is not really sad, she is just pretending to be
exercise: Which saying from India is now the country's national motto? | Truth alone triumphs | Might makes right in the end | Money talks louder than words | Every man for himself and his family
test: Your mate says, "Lying's fine as long as it's only once." What's the best answer? | One big lie can spend all the trust, and then even your true words get doubted | He's right, people always forget a single lie straight away | One lie is fine, but two lies is when it becomes a problem | Lying is only a problem if you get caught by a teacher
test: "Swear down, bruv, no cap, I seen a fox in the classroom," says Kieran. What does adding "no cap" tell you? | Nothing extra; a plain word should be enough, and over-swearing can sound less believable | It proves it is true, because nobody ever says "no cap" about a lie, not even as a joke in London | It means he is joking, because "no cap" means "this is a joke" | It means he is angry, because "cap" is a rude word in London
test: Grandad in Yorkshire says, "A man's word were his bond." What is "bond" here? | A promise you can rely on, like a signed agreement | A rope or a chain used to tie up the animals on a farm | A kind of money or savings that only grandparents use at the bank | A secret that you must never, ever tell anyone else
test: Your little sister says, "If I tell Mum I broke the plate, she'll go mental. Better to hide it." What is the best reply? | Mums are usually calmer when you tell them; a hidden plate becomes a lie too | Hide it really well under the bed, so that nobody will ever find the pieces or know what happened | Blame the cat, because cats cannot get into trouble | Throw it away in the neighbour's bin so it's not in our house
test: A cartoon shows a boy who cries "Wolf!" and the villagers always run to help, laughing. What is wrong with it? | In the fable people stop believing him, so when the wolf comes nobody helps | Nothing; that is exactly how the fable goes | The boy really cried "Bear!", not "Wolf!", in the fable | In the fable the wolf is a friendly dog who just wants to play
test: Aoife in Galway: "Yer man in the shop said the bike was grand, and the chain fell off." («Yer man» means «that man»; «grand» means «fine».) What does she mean? | That man in the shop said the bike was fine, but it wasn't | Her grandad said the bike was huge and very expensive to buy | The shop sold her a bike with no chain on it at all, so it couldn't go | Her friend said the bike was much too small and slow for her
test: A classmate who isn't religious asks, "Do you have to believe in God to think honesty matters?" What is the most accurate answer? | No; Christians see it as part of faith, but people of all beliefs agree trust needs honesty | Yes; only religious people care whether others tell the truth | No; nobody really thinks honesty matters, it is just a rule | Yes; honesty was invented by one particular church in England about a hundred years ago, before that people lied freely
test: "Shut up, it was only a cup, why's Mum making a big deal?" moans Sam. What is the best answer? | The cup isn't the big deal; the lie is, because now she can't believe you | You're right, Mum is just being dramatic about nothing | Buy her a new cup and then you never have to say sorry, because the cup was the only thing that mattered | Tell her the cat did it, then she'll calm down
test: What would happen if nobody in a town ever told the truth? | People could not trust shops, doctors, friends or even road signs | Life would be much more fun and exciting, and nobody would ever get upset about anything at all | Nothing would change, because people never listen anyway | Only the shops would suffer, and everyone else would be fine
test: Your friend says, "No one will see if I fill in the reading log wrong, so it doesn't count." Which story answers him best? | Plato's ring of Gyges: honesty is what you do when nobody can see | The three little pigs: build your house out of bricks | Cinderella: always be home before midnight | The hare and the tortoise: slow and steady wins the race, even when nobody is watching you
test: Destiny says, "Kayden says 'no cap' about everything and it's always cap." What does she mean? | Kayden says "no lie" a lot, but he is usually lying | Kayden never wears a hat or a cap, even when it is cold | Kayden is always telling the truth about everything he says | Kayden always forgets to bring his cap to school on hot days
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the relationship between honesty and trust? | Trust is constituted by an accumulation of truthful statements and may be forfeited by a single grave falsehood | Trust is conferred automatically upon all persons irrespective of their conduct | Trust is unaffected by falsehood provided that the falsehood is not discovered | Trust may be restored instantaneously by a single expression of apology
exam: Identify the commandment which, according to Exodus 20:16, protects the trust subsisting between neighbours. | You shall not bear false witness against your neighbour | You shall not covet your neighbour's house, nor anything that is your neighbour's | Honour your father and your mother | Remember the Sabbath day to keep it holy
exam: Select the response consistent with the teaching recorded in Matthew 5:37. | The plain affirmation or denial of an honest person ought to suffice without the addition of oaths | An affirmation is credible only when accompanied by a solemn oath | Oaths are to be employed whenever the speaker anticipates disbelief | Affirmations are binding solely when uttered within a place of worship
exam: Given that a child conceals a minor accident from a parent, ascertain the statement most consistent with the established reasoning of this lesson. | The concealment adds a falsehood to the original mistake and damages trust more than the accident | The concealment is commendable, the parent being thereby spared distress and the household preserved from needless anger | The concealment is of no consequence, the accident having been minor | The concealment is permissible provided that no third party observed the accident
